==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|Mephisto}}
* {{alter|en|Mephostopheles||dated}}

===Etymology===
Uncertain. The two-time occurrence of ''-ph-'' which is the Roman transliteration for Ancient Greek ''φ'' and on the termination ''-es'' which transliterates Ancient Greek ''-ης'' (e.g. found in ''Ἀριστοτέλης'') as well as the resemblance that ''-phel-/-phil-'' bears to {{m|grc|φιλέω|φιλεῖν|t=to love}} has led many to believe that this must originally be an Ancient Greek compound word.<ref name=":0">{{cite-book|en|last=Snider|first=Denton Jaques|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=um8oAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA132|title=Goethe's Faust: A Commentary|year=1886|publisher=Sigma|pages=132-133}}</ref> Following this assumption, the first two characters have been identified with {{m|grc|μή||not}}, while ''-phisto-''/''-phosto-'' has been interpreted as a corruption of {{m|grc||φωτο-}}, the compositional form of {{m|grc|φῶς|t=light}}. The name would thus mean "not loving light" which seems fitting for a devil. However, three obstacles stand in the way of this being acceptable: Firstly, ''-photo-'' is an unlikely candidate for corruption since this is a rather common and perspicuous Greek morpheme. Secondly, there are names and adjectives ending in {{m|grc|-φιλος}}, but none in {{m|grc||*-φίλης}}. Thirdly, {{m|grc|μή}} is not known to be part of Ancient Greek practice of name-composition. 

Instead of assuming two stems for the first part of the name, it has been proposed to identify it with Latin {{m|la|mephītis|t=poisonous exhalation from the ground}}, but the additional ''s'' still remains unexplained.

''-phosto-'' has formerly been interpreted as referring to {{m|en|Faust}} himself, in the sense that ''Mephostophiles'' "does not love Faust".

A semantically less straightforward, but morphologically more satisfying etymology postulates Hebrew origin,<ref>https://www.etymonline.com/word/Mephistopheles</ref> segmentating the name as מֵפִיץ (mêp̄îṣ) meaning {{m|en|scatterer}} and ט֫פֶל (tōp̄el) meaning {{m|en|attacher}}, followed by a suffix that turns the combination into a Greek word. ט֫פֶל would then be short for ט֫פֶל שֶׁ֫קֶר (tōp̄el šeqer) meaning ''attacher of lies'', a phrase found in Job 13,4: וְֽאוּלָ֗ם אַ֖תֶּם '''טֹֽפְלֵי־שָׁ֑קֶר''' רֹפְאֵ֖י אֱלִ֣ל כֻּלְּכֶֽם (translated as "ye are forgers of lies, ye are all quacks").

None of these etymologies is compelling.

